{
  "slug": "16bit-eiffel-tower",
  "title": "16-bit Eiffel Tower",
  "kind": "media",
  "prompt": "Create pixel art of the Eiffel Tower in the style of a Super Nintendo (SNES) game.\n\nFollow the SNES's limits:\n- 256\u00d7224 pixels, no upscaling\n- At most 128 colors in total\n- Use 8\u00d78 tiles, and keep each tile to 16 colors or fewer\n\nSave it as a PNG. Include a sky or background, and make it look like art from a polished 90s SNES game.",
  "final_prompt": "Create pixel art of the Eiffel Tower in the style of a Super Nintendo (SNES) game.\n\nFollow the SNES's limits:\n- 256\u00d7224 pixels, no upscaling\n- At most 128 colors in total\n- Use 8\u00d78 tiles, and keep each tile to 16 colors or fewer\n\nSave it as a PNG. Include a sky or background, and make it look like art from a polished 90s SNES game.\n\nDeliver the result as image and/or video files, working in the current directory.\n- Save the finished files in ./output/. Only what is in ./output/ is published as the result, so\n  ./output/ holds those finished image/video files and nothing else.\n- Formats: images as .png, .jpg, .webp, .gif or .svg; videos as .mp4 or .webm. At most 8 files,\n  each under 2 MB; videos at most 10 seconds.\n- Tools available on this machine: python3 3.9.6 (standard library only), node 26.11.1 (no npm packages), ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2. Don't install packages or download anything.\n- Keep the code you write to make the files in the working directory (its root, or a subdirectory\n  like ./src/, but not in ./output/): it's published as the source. Delete intermediate files such\n  as rendered frames when you're done.\n- Only create or edit files inside the current directory.\n- Finish with a short summary of what you made.\n",
